      "dom_id": "question-175915",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The equation of the line that passes through the points $(2,8)$ and $(4,4)$ is $\\,y=${{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-8}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-2$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=-2x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+b \\\\ 4 & =-2(4)+b \\\\ 4 & =-8+b \\\\ 4+8 & =b \\\\ 12 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the equation of the line is $y=-2\\,x+12.$",
